# .version-scope — which version-bearing paths are NOT this repo's release version.
#
# A SCOPE DECLARATION, NOT A MUTE SWITCH. Everything named here still has a version and
# still has to be right; it is just answerable on a DIFFERENT AXIS, so requiring it to
# equal this repo's release number would be asking two questions and accepting one answer.
# Whatever is excluded is printed on every run, including a clean one.
#
# This repo's release version lives in exactly two places, both in scope:
#   VERSION            the number of record
#   CHANGELOG.md       the top version heading, which must agree with it
#
# ── the three extension cells ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `languages/rust/extensions/<ext>/Cargo.toml` carries the EXTENSION SPECIFICATION REVISION
# the cell was authored against, not a number about this repo:
#
#   content  3.6.0    written against EXTENSION-CONTENT v3.6
#   history  1.7.0    written against EXTENSION-HISTORY v1.7
#   compute  3.29.0   written against EXTENSION-COMPUTE v3.29
#
# Every one of them is `publish = false`. A cell is staged out of this tree and compiled
# inside a generated peer; nothing is distributed under these names and nothing outside the
# tree resolves them. The `typescript` and `python` cells carry no manifest at all — theirs
# is synthesised at composition time — so `rust` is the only target where this axis is even
# visible on disk, which is why three files appear here and nine cells do not.
#
# ⚠ TWO OF THE THREE ARE STALE AGAINST THE CURRENT PIN. `shared/spec-data/` is pinned at
# EXTENSION-CONTENT v3.7 and EXTENSION-HISTORY v1.10, and neither cell version moved with
# the re-pin. This declaration does NOT fix that, and is not meant to: it says the
# disagreement is with the extension contract, not with the release. Only `compute` (3.29.0
# against v3.29) currently states its revision correctly.
#
# The glob, not the three paths: the axis belongs to "an extension cell", so a fourth
# extension or a fourth target inherits the ruling instead of silently refusing a cut.
languages/*/extensions/*/Cargo.toml

# ── the gate probe crates ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `languages/rust/gates/<gate>/Cargo.toml` are the probes and their compile-fail controls —
# `host-seam`, `chunking-parity`, `type-parity`. All `publish = false`, all built only by
# the gate driver that expects their result, none reachable from outside this tree. They
# read `0.0.0` deliberately: a probe makes a claim about a peer, never about itself, and a
# number there would be one more thing to keep true for no reader.
languages/*/gates/*/Cargo.toml
